3.6 while
int i = 0;

while (i < 5)
{
    printf("%d ", i);
    i++;
}

The condition is checked before executing the body.

Therefore while is a:

Entry-controlled loop

3.7 do-while
int i = 0;

do
{
    printf("%d ", i);
    i++;
}
while (i < 5);

The body executes first.

Therefore:

do-while = Exit-controlled loop

VERY IMPORTANT EXAM FACT

A do-while loop executes at least once, even if its condition is initially false.

3.8 break

break immediately terminates the loop/switch.

Example:

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
{
    if (i == 3)
        break;

    printf("%d ", i);
}

Output:

1 2
3.9 continue

continue skips the current iteration and proceeds to the next iteration.

Example:

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
{
    if (i == 3)
        continue;

    printf("%d ", i);
}

Output:

1 2 4 5
Memory trick

