PART C — RECURSION

This is HIGH PRIORITY.

Recursion means:

A function calls itself.

Example:

void fun()
{
    fun();
}

This is recursion, but it has a problem: there is no stopping condition.

Therefore, recursion normally needs a base case.

Example: factorial

Mathematically:

5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1

Therefore:

5! = 120

Recursive definition:

n! = n × (n-1)!

with:

0! = 1

C:

int factorial(int n)
{
    if (n == 0)
        return 1;

    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

For:

factorial(3)

execution becomes:

factorial(3)
= 3 × factorial(2)

= 3 × 2 × factorial(1)

= 3 × 2 × 1 × factorial(0)

= 3 × 2 × 1 × 1

= 6
Two essential parts of recursion
1. Base case
2. Recursive case

Without a base case, recursion may continue until stack overflow.

